WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.959 --> 00:00:07.920
Two of the biggest films of 2016—Captain
America: Civil War and Batman v Superman:

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:12.519
Dawn of Justice—had nearly the same plot.
This isn’t the first time Hollywood made

00:00:12.519 --> 00:00:17.320
two movies with the same story at the same
time. How does this happen? And which of the

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:20.249
movies did fans like more? Let's find out.

00:00:20.249 --> 00:00:23.140
Dawn of Justice vs. Civil War

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:27.529
Even though Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice
and Captain America: Civil War may have come

00:00:27.529 --> 00:00:31.929
out at nearly the same time, large chunks
of each were based on comic books published

00:00:31.929 --> 00:00:37.410
20 years apart: DC’s The Dark Knight Returns
came out in 1986 and Marvel’s Civil War

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:39.579
came out in 2006.

00:00:39.579 --> 00:00:43.649
Dawn of Justice got the jump by arriving in
theaters first. But when it comes to battles

00:00:43.649 --> 00:00:48.420
between superhero films about battles between
superheroes, Captain America: Civil War is

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:53.570
the clear winner. Not only did it easily blow
past Batman v Superman's box office take in

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:59.070
a matter of days, it also has a 90% fresh
rating on Rotten Tomatoes compared to just

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:03.280
27% for Batman v Superman. Pow!

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:05.680
The Equalizer vs. John Wick

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:10.979
The Equalizer and John Wick arrived in theaters
less than a month apart in the fall of 2014.

00:01:10.979 --> 00:01:15.140
And they shared a couple specific, major plot
points: both have a lead character who is

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:19.780
a retired hitman dealing with the death of
a beloved wife, and both come out of retirement

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:24.229
for personal reasons to exact revenge on the
Russian mob. What kind of monsters kill a

00:01:24.229 --> 00:01:25.439
guy’s dog?!

00:01:25.439 --> 00:01:29.299
The similarities didn't seem to affect the
performance of either movie. Both were box

00:01:29.299 --> 00:01:31.930
office hits and paved the way for sequels.

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:34.549
"I'm thinkin' I'm back!"

00:01:34.549 --> 00:01:36.759
Olympus Has Fallen vs. White House Down

00:01:36.759 --> 00:01:42.000
Back in 2013, it was no secret that both Sony
and Millennium Films were making movies about

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.299
terrorists attacking the White House. The
competing projects publicly raced each other

00:01:46.299 --> 00:01:51.070
to see which could land the bigger stars and
get released first. Agent Gerard Butler and

00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:55.340
President Aaron Eckhart hit theaters first
with Olympus Has Fallen, with agent Channing

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:59.259
Tatum and President Jamie Foxx following just
a couple months later in White House Down.

00:01:59.259 --> 00:02:03.329
"Ow! Can you not hit me in the head with a
rocket while I'm trying to drive!"

00:02:03.329 --> 00:02:07.939
Both films were moderate successes at the
box office, but neither hit it big. Though

00:02:07.939 --> 00:02:13.220
Olympus Has Fallen got a poorly reviewed sequel
in 2016: London Has Fallen. Turns out fictional

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:17.050
politics just can’t match the entertainment
factor of the real thing.

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:19.830
No Strings Attached vs. Friends with Benefits

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:24.300
Paramount and Screen Gems had a bit of a pickle
on their hands in 2011. Not only were they

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:28.690
both making movies about couples who start
off casual only to fall in love, but both

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:33.380
films had the same name: Friends with Benefits.
Paramount eventually blinked, changing the

00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:38.450
name of their movie to No Strings Attached.
It barely mattered. Both Friends with Benefits

00:02:38.450 --> 00:02:41.990
and No Strings Attached were huge hits at
the box office.

00:02:41.990 --> 00:02:45.640
Apparently it was an idea whose time had come.
Twice.

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:48.140
Megamind vs. Despicable Me

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:52.870
It's easy to see the similarities between
2010's Despicable Me and Megamind: they're

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:57.100
both animated features about supervillains
who aren't really bad guys at all, which they

00:02:57.100 --> 00:03:01.450
prove after stopping an actual villain from
conquering everything. And the critics took

00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:06.140
notice, hammering Megamind for being too similar
to Despicable Me, which had opened earlier

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:10.500
in the year. But the main difference that
turned Despicable Me into a worldwide phenomenon

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:13.160
wasn't timing, it was those lovable minions.

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:17.900
It just goes to show that the old Hollywood
adage is still true: never mess with a talking

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:20.090
Twinkie.

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:22.070
Skyline vs. Battle: Los Angeles

00:03:22.070 --> 00:03:26.350
These two films about aliens attacking LA
were just a little too similar for Battle:

00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:31.430
Los Angeles producer Sony Pictures. They threatened
to sue Skyline directors Colin and Greg Strouse

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:35.440
because the company they used for Skyline's
special effects had also been hired by Sony

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:40.220
to do special effects for Battle: Los Angeles.
Sony alleged that the Strouse brothers had

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:44.620
stolen the idea from them—or at least some
of the special effects—but eventually decided

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:48.910
not to bother suing. They probably figured
Los Angeles couldn't survive another battle

00:03:48.910 --> 00:03:50.240
so soon.

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:52.570
The Prestige vs. The Illusionist

00:03:52.570 --> 00:03:57.400
It's one thing when Hollywood coughs up two
films about general concepts like alien invasions.

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:01.790
It's another when two films come out at the
same time about a highly specific topic, like

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:06.980
the world of stage magicians in the late 19th
century. Somehow, though, that's exactly what

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:10.280
happened in 2006 with The Prestige and The
Illusionist.

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:11.540
"What happened?"

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:16.460
"It was the greatest magic trick I've ever
seen"

00:04:16.759 --> 00:04:21.139
Both featured stellar casts and both did well
at the box office. But it was The Prestige

00:04:21.139 --> 00:04:25.310
that earned the prestige, pulling two Oscar
nominations out of its hat compared to just

00:04:25.310 --> 00:04:26.730
one for The Illusionist.

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:28.130
(applause)

00:04:28.130 --> 00:04:29.530
"Bravo!"

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.719
Deep Impact vs. Armageddon

00:04:31.719 --> 00:04:36.469
Finally, there's Deep Impact and Armageddon.
Fueled by fears that the end of the millennium

00:04:36.469 --> 00:04:41.310
would also bring about the end of the world,
audiences in 1998 were primed for films about

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:45.800
giant asteroids crashing into Earth, and both
films became box office smashes.

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:54.349
"Rejoice in what we have been re-given. Our
planet."

00:04:54.349 --> 00:04:58.280
Most people probably don't even remember Deep
Impact, though, while Armageddon has entered

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:03.080
legend as one of the cheesiest blockbusters
of all time. Hard to believe, but Deep Impact

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:04.640
just wasn't quite dumb enough.

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:16.319
Of course, that stupid Aerosmith song playing
on endless repeat on the radio since 1998

00:05:16.319 --> 00:05:21.370
probably helped ensure that we'd never, ever
forget Armageddon for as long as we lived...

00:05:21.370 --> 00:05:27.180
(Aerosmith song playing)

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:30.800
Thanks for watching! Subscribe to our YouTube
channel to watch more videos like the one

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:34.879
you just saw. And leave a comment to let us
know which movies you think won the battle...

